sys/include/ape/c++ -- the C++ language-support headers.

WHAT THESE ARE.  EDG's own `include_c++' set, copied verbatim from
`sys/src/external/edg/include_c++'.  Its upstream README says exactly
why it exists: "only those headers that require specific magic to work
right with the EDG front end."  They are the LANGUAGE-SUPPORT set --
<new>, <typeinfo>, <exception>, <stdexcept>, <initializer_list>,
<compare>, <coroutine>, <source_location>, <stdfloat>, plus `cxxabi.h'
and `edg_internal_defines' -- and a grep across all of them for
`basic_string|vector|iostream|ostream' is EMPTY.

**THERE IS NO C++ STANDARD LIBRARY HERE AND EDG SHIPS NONE.**  What
works with no library at all is the subset EDG itself is written in:
classes, templates, virtual dispatch, RAII, new/delete, plus
`bad_alloc', `type_info' and `initializer_list' from these headers.  The
matching ABI runtime -- operator new/delete, vtables, RTTI, static
initialisation, pure-virtual -- is `libedg.a', built from
`sys/src/ape/lib/edg'.  That is the libsupc++/libc++abi layer, not
libstdc++.  See `sys/src/external/edg/NOTE'.

WHY `.stdh'.  `src/cmd_line.c:23289' sets
`include_file_suffixes = "::stdh:"' -- an empty suffix, then `stdh' --
so cpfe resolves `#include <new>' to `new.stdh' through its own suffix
list.  **No other compiler in the tree can read those files**, and that
is fine here: they are only ever reached through `eccp''s
`--sys_include=/sys/include/ape/c++'.  It is also why
`sys/lib/tests/apehdr-sweep.py' does not report them -- that sweep is
`os.listdir' over `sys/include/ape' and is TOP LEVEL ONLY, so this
subdirectory has always been outside it.  (An earlier round recorded the
opposite as a reason not to use this directory.  It was wrong about
that, and measuring the sweep's scope is what said so.)

CFRONT USED TO OWN THIS DIRECTORY.  Seventeen pre-standard headers --
`iostream.h', `stream.h', `strstream.h', `vector.h' and the rest -- went
when cfront was removed from the tree.  **Two names collided with this
set and one of them collided twice**: cfront's `new.h' DECLARED
`operator new' and carried `#pragma lib "ape/libc++.a"' where EDG's is a
one-line shim doing `#include <new>', **and both used the include guard
`__NEW_H'**, so whichever was found first silently erased the other.
`stdexcept.h' was the second name.  That is the `Lock'
duplicate-typedef shape and the `reject' shape in one file, and it is
the reason these two sets could never have shared a directory.

THE COPIES CAN DRIFT, AND THIS IS THE CHECK.  Two copies of a header
that may disagree is the `PATH_MAX'/`limits_generic.h' class, and the
`f2c.h' question was settled in one command by diffing the installed
copy against the one next door.  From the repository root:

	diff -rq -x README sys/include/ape/c++ sys/src/external/edg/include_c++

**EMPTY OUTPUT IS THE PASS**, which is why `-q' and the exclusion are
both there rather than a bare `diff -r': `-q' names the files that
differ instead of printing seventy lines of their contents, and README
is excluded because this one REPLACES upstream's rather than copying it.
Any line at all means a re-import landed in one place and not the other.

`include_c99' IS NOT MIRRORED HERE and needs no equivalent.  It holds
one file, `iso646.h', and `sys/include/ape/iso646.h' is ours now --
reached by `eccp''s `--sys_include=/sys/include/ape' -- and is the
better of the two, since it is guarded `#ifndef __cplusplus' where
EDG's defines the eleven macros unconditionally.  In C++ they are
KEYWORDS, and `<ciso646>' has been empty since C++98.

THE HEADER-ONLY STL IS **NOT** HERE, and the reason is not space.
`sys/src/external/paragon_portable_stl' is 720 headers with zero system
includes, which makes it the best C++ corpus this tree has -- and it is
`namespace portable_stl', not `std', with `vector::push_back' returning
`expected<void, portable_stl_error>' where C++ says `void'.  *A renamed
STL serves NEW code; only a `std::' one serves a PORT*, and no aliasing
recovers an API that differs.  Installing it here would put something
called a standard library where `#include <vector>' does not find one --
the capability-present-and-not-declared bug with the sign reversed.
It stays where it is, reached by `eccp''s `$STLINCL'.  If it is ever
installed it wants `sys/include/ape/c++/portable_stl/', since its own
includes are spelled `portable_stl/...' and the one `--sys_include'
above already covers that.
